1. The 10-Second Kubernetes Pod Pipeline
[Certain] Never run random commands; 
follow this strict triage flow based on what kubectl get pods shows
:If Pending THEN Scheduling or Volume FailureRun: kubectl describe pod <pod-name> and jump straight to the Events section at the bottom
.Look for: Insufficient cpu/memory (lower your requests), FailedMount (missing PVC/Secret), or unmatched nodeSelector.
If CrashLoopBackOff $\rightarrow$ Process Exited PrematurelyRun: kubectl logs <pod-name> --previousLook for: Missing environment variables, syntax errors in code, or database connection refusals.
If logs are empty, check if the container command was just a short-lived script without a background daemon.If ImagePullBackOff / ErrImagePull $\rightarrow$ Registry or Typo Error
Run: kubectl describe pod <pod-name>Look for: Image name typos, missing tags (e.g., using :v1 instead of :latest), or missing imagePullSecrets for private registries.
If CreateContainerConfigError $\rightarrow$ Missing ConfigMap / SecretRun: kubectl get configmap,secretLook for: A typo in the configMapKeyRef.name or secretKeyRef.name referenced in the pod spec.
If Running but Unreachable $\rightarrow$ Routing DisconnectRun: kubectl get endpoints <service-name>Look for: Empty endpoints (<none>). 
This guarantees spec.selector in your Service does not match metadata.labels on your Pod, or targetPort doesn't match the container's listening port.
2. The 3-Rule Syntax Scan (Zero Execution Required)
[Certain] Catch 80% of CLI and Dockerfile bugs instantly by checking these exact boundaries:
Rule 1: Left is Outside, Right is InsidePorts: -p <hostPort>:<containerPort>. If the app listens on 8080, 8080 must be on the right side of the colon.Volumes: -v <hostPath>:<containerPath>:Z.
If you see a system folder like /usr/share/nginx/html on the left side, the mount is reversed.
Rule 2: Match Package Manager to Base OSCheck line 1 (FROM <image>). 
If line 1 is alpine, any use of apt-get or yum fails immediately.ubuntu/debian = apt-get | alpine = apk | rhel/fedora = dnf.
Rule 3: Check Array vs. Shell SyntaxAlways ensure CMD and ENTRYPOINT use JSON arrays: ["python3", "app.py"].
If written as unquoted text (CMD python3 app.py), it spawns inside /bin/sh -c, which traps execution and prevents graceful shutdowns (SIGTERM).
3. Podman & CLI Rapid IsolatorInstant Exited (0): [Certain] The container ran a command that finished immediately (like echo or busybox).
Fix by appending a blocking command: sleep infinity.Disappearing Containers: [Certain] If --rm is combined with -d, any startup crash deletes the container instantly before you can check logs. 
Remove --rm while debugging.Ignored Port Flags: [Certain] If --network host is passed alongside -p 8080:80, the -p flag is ignored completely because host networking bypasses port mapping.
Permission Denied on Mounts: [Certain] On SELinux systems, host folders mounted via -v require :Z (private) or :z (shared) appended at the end to grant container access permissions.
4. High-Speed Copy-Paste CommandsBash# Sort cluster events by time to see the exact second everything broke
kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp


# Get clean restart counts and termination exit codes (Look for 137 = OOMKilled)
kubectl get pods -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{"\tExitCode: "}{.status.containerStatuses[0].lastState.terminated.exitCode}{"\tReason: "}{.status.containerStatuses[0].lastState.terminated.reason}{"\n"}{end}'

# Test service DNS and TCP ports directly from inside the cluster
kubectl run tmp --rm -it --image=busybox -- /bin/sh -c "nslookup <svc-name> && nc -zv <svc-name> <port>"

# Inject a debugging shell into a running distroless or broken container
kubectl debug -it <pod-name> --image=busybox --target=<container-name>

# Validate YAML syntax and structural bugs without applying to cluster
kubectl apply -f file.yaml --dry-run=server --validate=true
